ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vagal Nerve Dysfunction / Low Vagal Tone - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Autonomic Flare)
Differentials:

Acute Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Auscultated rhythm is distinctly regular sinus tachycardia that fluctuates with respiration, rather than a fixed, sudden-onset electrical arrhythmia >150 bpm.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain/tightness is vague/mild rather than crushing central pressure, clear correlation with severe anxiety/hyperventilation, and normal oxygenation.

Acute Upper Airway Obstruction / Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear visualization of the oropharynx, absence of stridor, normal voice quality, and normal oxygen saturations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe resting tachycardia, subjective respiratory distress, and a choking sensation. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent, time-critical need to evaluate for a life-threatening acute cardiac arrhythmia, acute myocardial ischemia, or an obstructing airway lesion. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to auscultate the heart and lungs, visually inspect the airway, and measure oxygen saturation. The physical confirmation of a regular sinus rhythm, clear lungs, and a patent airway confirmed the absence of a primary electrical arrhythmia or immediate respiratory failure. However, the severity of the dysautonomia, the intense choking sensation, and the unbearable psychological panic dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a distinct, unacceptable risk of syncopal head trauma (from hyperventilation), severe dehydration (from refusal to swallow), and immense physical suffering. This urgent intervention unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Diazepam 2.5mg stat orally to immediately blunt the severe autonomic hyperarousal, lower the heart rate, and relax the cricopharyngeal muscle spasm causing the globus sensation. Guided the patient through physiological vagal maneuvers. Recommend urgent GP follow-up for a formal cardiology referral (Holter monitor) and functional assessment of vagal tone (HRV monitoring).
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically manage the acute vagal dysfunction and sympathetic overdrive, initiate strict mechanical and respiratory protocols. Action: Perform the "mammalian dive reflex" maneuver by submerging the face in a bowl of ice-cold water for 10 seconds whenever the heart racing or panic begins. Consequence: The intense cold on the trigeminal nerve directly and forcefully stimulates the vagus nerve, explicitly overriding the sympathetic "fight or flight" loop and rewarding the patient with an immediate, rapid drop in heart rate. Action: Implement a daily practice of resonant frequency breathing (inhaling for 4 seconds, exhaling for 6 seconds) for 10 minutes every morning. Consequence: Extending the expiratory phase mechanically physically tones the vagal nerve over time, directly improving respiratory sinus arrhythmia and rewarding the patient with a higher baseline resistance to acute stress flares.
